module bindings.ruby.oauth_error_response_redirect_uri_without_preauth;

// An OAuth authorization server answers a malformed authorization request by
// redirecting the browser back with the error in the query string, and the
// request's own redirect_uri parameter decides where that redirect goes. The
// value is only safe to echo once the server has matched it against the
// client's registered redirect URIs; doorkeeper performs that match in the
// PreAuthorization object the host controller exposes as `pre_auth`, whose
// documented `valid?` predicate is the gate. An error handler that builds its
// response from the raw parameter and redirects to it before that gate has
// run lets whoever can trigger the error choose the destination, because the
// error itself is what makes the validation fail.
//
// The two `field:` conjuncts carry the shape: a request parameter read placed
// under a `redirect_uri` key (`field:redirect_uri=params[...`, the
// authorization-request spelling of the pair) together with a `location:`
// keyword (`field:location=...`, the Rails `render`/`redirect_to` spelling of
// "set the Location header"). Both are AST-derived pair tokens and stay
// confined to the function that carries them -- a sibling method constructing
// an error response without the parameter, or rendering without the location
// keyword, satisfies neither. The gate is the negation: doorkeeper's
// `pre_auth.valid?`, the documented predicate that the redirect_uri was
// validated against the registered client. A remediation keeps the same
// construction and redirect, and only guards the construction with the gate,
// so the negation is what separates them; the same predicate run elsewhere in
// the file also silences the finding, because `call_path:` tokens at function
// scope are file-wide in Ruby. That leak is a false-negative direction only:
// it takes a validation call to silence, and prose cannot produce one.
//
// Residuals: a project that validates the redirect_uri under a different
// spelling (`authorizable?`, a helper) keeps the finding, and a redirect that
// spells the target positionally (`redirect_to params[:redirect_uri]`) is the
// taint rule's `RedirectTargetSink` shape, not this one.

binding oauthErrorResponseRedirectUriWithoutPreauth {
  query pattern presenceNode where node.scope == "function"
    and node.context.language == "ruby"
    and node.context.field contains "field:redirect_uri=params["
    and node.context.field contains "field:location="
    and not (node.context.callPath contains "pre_auth.valid?")
  emit issue code.OauthErrorResponseRedirectUriWithoutPreauth at node
}
